.TH iio_daqiri_bench 1 "@CMAKE_DATE@" "libiio-@LIBIIO_VERSION_MAJOR@.@LIBIIO_VERSION_MINOR@" "LibIIO Utilities"
.IX iio_daqiri_bench
.SH NAME
iio_daqiri_bench \- measure the receive throughput of an IIO buffer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iio_daqiri_bench
[
.I options
]
.B \-d
.I device
.B \-s
.I block_bytes
.B \-T
.I seconds
[\-c <ch1,ch2>] [\-n <blocks>] [\-\-check ramp|none] [\-j <file.json>]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iio_daqiri_bench
queues a number of blocks on the first input buffer of a device, starts the
stream, and dequeues and re-queues them for the requested time, reporting the
byte and block rates, the dequeue latency, the CPU use and, for
.B daqiri:
contexts, the data path the buffer took and the buffer counters.
.PP
It only uses the public libiio API, so the same run works against any backend:
compare
.B daqiri:
with
.B ip:
on the same sender.
.PP
The clock starts when the first block is dequeued. That block is checked but
not counted, so the time spent waiting for the sender to start is not part of
the rate. Dequeue latency percentiles cover every dequeue, the first one
included. The CPU percentage is the process total (user plus system, all
threads) over the measured time; on Linux, the figure of every thread follows.
.PP
The counters are the
.B daqiri_*
buffer attributes. They are read right after the stream started and again at
the end, and the report holds the difference, so packets that were dropped
before the stream started are not counted. They are shown only when the buffer
has the
.B daqiri_rx_*
attributes.
.SH OPTIONS
##COMMON_COMMANDS_START##
##COMMON_COMMANDS_STOP##
##COMMON_OPTION_START##
##COMMON_OPTION_STOP##
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-device <device>
The device to receive from (required).
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-channels <ch1,ch2>
Comma separated channels to enable. They must be scan elements of the first
buffer. The default is all of them.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-block\-size <bytes>
Size of a block in bytes (required). It must be a multiple of the sample size.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-blocks <count>
Number of blocks queued (default: 8).
.TP
.B \-T, \-\-duration <seconds>
How long to measure, from the first block (required). Unlike in the other
utilities,
.B \-T
is not the context timeout: use
.B \-\-timeout
for that.
.TP
.B \-k, \-\-check ramp|none
.B ramp
verifies the ramp16_le test pattern (sample n has I = n & 0x7FFF and
Q = (0x8000 - n) & 0xFFFF, little-endian 16-bit words, as the test pattern of
the board's UDP framer sends them; see ramp16_le in tests/daqiri/gen_golden.py)
on host-order int16 I/Q pairs, and counts the samples that do not follow it.
It needs 4-byte samples. After a dequeue error (lost data) the pattern is
joined again from the next block. The default is
.BR none .
.TP
.B \-j, \-\-json <file>
Also write the result to this file as JSON.
.SH OUTPUT
The text report holds
.I data_path
(for
.B daqiri:
contexts: the
.B daqiri_data_path
buffer attribute,
.B udp
or
.BR tcp ,
the data path settled when the buffer was opened),
.IR bytes ,
.IR blocks ,
.IR gbps ,
.IR blocks_per_s ,
.IR dequeue_p50_ms ,
.IR dequeue_p99_ms ,
.I cpu_pct
(\fBn/a\fR when the platform cannot tell) and, with
.BR "\-\-check ramp" ,
.IR bad_samples .
.PP
The JSON file is an object with
.IR schema_version " (1),"
.IR uri ,
.IR device ,
.I data_path
(\fBnull\fR when the buffer has no \fBdaqiri_data_path\fR),
.IR block_bytes ,
.IR duration_s ,
.IR bytes ,
.IR blocks ,
.IR gbps ,
.IR blocks_per_s ,
.I dequeue_ms
(\fBp50\fR, \fBp99\fR),
.I cpu_pct
(\-1 when unknown),
.I counters
(the \fBdaqiri_*\fR attributes; the \fBdaqiri_rx_*\fR and \fBdaqiri_blocks_*\fR
ones as differences, except \fBdaqiri_rx_socket_buffer\fR, a size; the others
as read) and
.I check
(\fBmode\fR, \fBbad_samples\fR).
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns 0 when the run completed, and a non-zero exit code when the device or
channels are not found, the buffer cannot be opened, no block arrived, or a
dequeue failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
iio_daqiri_bench \-u "daqiri:192.168.2.1,data=udp,yaml=/etc/daqiri.yaml" \\
    \-d cf\-ad9361\-lpc \-s 1440 \-T 5 \-\-check ramp \-j perf.json
iio_daqiri_bench \-u ip:192.168.2.1 \-d cf\-ad9361\-lpc \-s 65536 \-T 3
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iio_rwdev (1)
